[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports hold three
problems. The queued parser fix closes one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Three problems, three tickets. Fixing the parser leaves the other two in place.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the
  day first. Day and month swap: "7 April" goes out as "4 July". Any channel except a booking-v3 form.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type month first, as
  told, and the form reads the date day first, the tenant's way. Only form reports from booking-v3
  tenants.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by exactly one day, mostly back: "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday",
  "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." A month-first parser swaps day and
  month; it cannot move a date by one day. This points at time zones. These are the reports the desk
  already read as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`).

## How the merge hid them

- **Subjects.** [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. The same
  subjects come in for different problems, so all three landed in one ticket.
- **Intake tags.** The tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the tenant: its
  locale and its booking form. Count by tag and the split is wrong.
- **Repeats.** The ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice. Count one
  report per id.
- **Footnotes.** The kb's cause table files the one-day moves under `parser` by tenant and footnotes
  them. Their bodies describe a different bug.

